      balanced by numbers alone (per-band spectra, not ears).

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    what you see
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      night. open sea. no moon.
      one small boat with one lantern, and a lighthouse
      miles off that sweeps its beam through the fog.

      the light does not rescue anyone. it keeps the watch.

      0:00   black water, the lighthouse dark
      0:15   the lamp comes on
      0:27   the beam crosses the boat; the hull goes black
      0:44   the swell deepens; the beam's pool widens
      1:04   the sweep finds the others: twelve lanterns
      1:16   the camera climbs; the fleet is out there
      1:40   the watch
      2:08   end

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    how to run
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      Double-click intro.html in Edge or Chrome, wait for the
      title screen to finish loading, then click.
      No server, no flags. ESC leaves fullscreen.
      A discrete GPU is recommended. Tested on an RTX 5090.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    how it is made
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      - the beam is a closed-form line integral of a gaussian
        cone through the fog, not a marched volume: no ray can
        slip past it and there is no step to undersample. The
        same integral lights the sea, the reflection and every
        fleet lantern
      - the sea is one plane with six gravity-dispersed waves
        on a golden angle, so the normal is exact; it carries
        the beam's torn path, the pool where the cone lands,
        and the tower's and boat's reflections as streaks
      - the tower and the boat are flat cut-outs drawn nearly
        black on the fog; the lantern's own warm light is the
        only thing that plays on them
      - post: halation above a threshold, ACES, vignette, and
        grain seeded by the frame, weighted to the shadows
      - the soundtrack is a fragment shader: one pixel per
        stereo sample at 48 kHz, 90 BPM in 3/4, a barcarolle
        in D minor. Cello tune, harp waltz, foghorn, bell-buoy,
        sea, a chime cluster for the fleet. One bar is 2 s, so
        the beam's 16 s revolution is exactly 8 bars
      - the room is a 4-line Householder FDN in JavaScript
      - packed with zopfli into an HTML comment and unpacked by
        DecompressionStream, so it runs from file://

      Source, build tools and notes are in this folder; see
      readme.md.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    thanks
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      No assets or libraries are included. Techniques: Inigo
      Quilez (value noise and the distance-field canon),
      Krzysztof Narkowicz (the ACES fit used in post), and the
      FDN reverb structure shared by all Latent intros. The
      beam integral is standard analytic geometry: the closest
      approach of a ray to an axis, then a gaussian in the
      perpendicular distance.

      And to whoever kept the light on, on a night nobody
      was watching.

